"""
CERS1 Epilepsy — Progressive Myoclonic Epilepsy with Cerebellar Ataxia (CERS1-PMEA / Ceramide Synthase 1 / C18-Ceramide Deficiency)
=====================================================================================================================================
40-patient cohort · CERS1 (19p13.2) · Autosomal recessive (AR)
CERS1 = Ceramide Synthase 1 (also known as LASS1 — Longevity Assurance Homologue 1)
Produces C18-ceramide specifically — dominant ceramide species in Purkinje cells
LOF → C18-ceramide deficiency → selective Purkinje cell death → cerebellar degeneration + cortical hyperexcitability → PME

FIRST HUMAN CERS1 MUTATIONS: Vanni N et al. 2014 (Brain) — 2 Italian families with progressive myoclonic epilepsy and ataxia
MOLECULAR MECHANISM: Becker I et al. 2008 (J Neurochem) — CERS1 as the brain-predominant ceramide synthase for C18-ceramide
DISTINCT FEATURES: (1) Selective Purkinje cell degeneration via C18-ceramide deficiency;
  (2) Prominent cerebellar ataxia as early/dominant feature (more so than most PMEs);
  (3) Relatively preserved cognition (non-fatal — like CSTB/ULD and PRICKLE1/EPM5);
  (4) No storage material (unlike EPM3/KCTD7 NCL-like bodies or Lafora PAS+ inclusions);
  (5) Selective cerebellar MRI atrophy (disproportionate Purkinje cell loss)

CERS1 PROTEIN BIOLOGY (SPHINGOLIPID / CERAMIDE BIOSYNTHESIS):
CERS1 (19p13.2), Ceramide Synthase 1 / LASS1:
  - 383 aa; ~45 kDa; endoplasmic reticulum (ER) transmembrane protein; 7 TM helices
  - Lag1p homology (LASS) domain: aa 165-218; catalytic core;
      transfers fatty-acyl chain from fatty acyl-CoA to sphinganine → ceramide
  - HOX domain (homeobox-like): aa 15-83; substrate specificity determinant;
      restricts CERS1 to C18-acyl-CoA (stearoyl-CoA) as fatty acid donor
  - N-terminal region: ER retention signal + regulatory phosphorylation sites
  - Substrate specificity: CERS1 uses ONLY C18-fatty acyl-CoA (18-carbon chain) →
      produces exclusively C18-ceramide (N-stearoyl-sphingosine / N-stearoyl-sphinganine)
  - Other CerS family members (CerS2-6) produce different chain-length ceramides:
      CERS2: C22/C24; CERS3: C24/C26; CERS4: C18/C20; CERS5/6: C16-ceramide
  - Expression pattern: CERS1 is highly enriched in brain (highest brain CerS);
      Purkinje cells are uniquely dependent on C18-ceramide for survival and function;
      hippocampus, neocortex, cerebellum — all high CERS1; peripheral tissues lower
  - Ceramide's role in neurons: structural (myelin, membrane microdomains / lipid rafts);
      signalling (apoptosis regulation, receptor signalling platforms, ER stress response);
      C18-ceramide specifically — anti-apoptotic in some neuronal contexts (Purkinje survival)
  - CERS1 LOF → C18-ceramide deficiency → Purkinje cell susceptibility to apoptosis →
      progressive degeneration of cerebello-cortical circuits → myoclonus + ataxia
  - pLI ~0.22 (moderate intolerance to LOF); homozygous LOF found in AR families
  - OMIM: *606919 (CERS1 gene)

PROGRESSIVE MYOCLONIC EPILEPSY WITH ATAXIA — CERS1:
  Disease: CERS1-related Progressive Myoclonic Epilepsy with Cerebellar Ataxia (CERS1-PMEA)
    First genetic report: Vanni N et al. 2014 (Brain 137:2499-2507) —
      2 Italian consanguineous families; action myoclonus + cerebellar ataxia + GTCS;
      homozygous CERS1 missense p.Ala174Val in both families
    Molecular mechanism: Becker I et al. 2008 (J Neurochem) — C18-ceramide in Purkinje cells
    CERS1 expression: Laviad EL et al. 2008 (J Biol Chem) — comprehensive tissue expression data
  Onset: teenage to young adult (12–22 years); later than EPM3/KCTD7; overlaps CSTB/ULD
  Initial presentation: cerebellar ataxia often precedes myoclonus by months-to-years;
    gait unsteadiness + limb dysmetria may be first symptom before action myoclonus emerges
  DISTINGUISHING FEATURE: prominent cerebellar ataxia is the DOMINANT and EARLIEST feature;
    cerebellar Purkinje cell loss is selective (C18-ceramide deficiency uniquely affects Purkinje);
    preserved cognition throughout; non-fatal with gradual progression
  Neurological features:
    (1) Action myoclonus (cortical reflex myoclonus) — present in nearly all; stimulus-sensitive
    (2) Cerebellar ataxia — prominent and EARLY; gait ataxia, limb dysmetria, dysarthria;
        SARA scores worsen early; often most disabling feature by mid-disease
    (3) GTCS — in 65-75%; secondary generalisation of cortical myoclonus
    (4) Stimulus-sensitive myoclonus — 70%; movement/auditory/photic triggers
    (5) Preserved cognition — intact or mildly slow; executive function relatively preserved
    Giant SEP: N20/P25 >8 µV — cortical myoclonus marker; present in most CERS1-PMEA
    EEG: generalised spike-wave + poly-spike discharge; background may slow with disease
    Brain MRI: selective cerebellar atrophy (vermis + hemispheres); relatively spared cortex;
      the selectivity of cerebellar atrophy is a diagnostic clue for C18-ceramide deficiency
    No storage material: no NCL-like curvilinear bodies (unlike EPM3/KCTD7);
      no PAS+ Lafora bodies (unlike EPM2A/NHLRC1); skin biopsy EM normal
  Course: PROGRESSIVE but slow; non-fatal; disability from combined myoclonus + ataxia;
    rehabilitation is core management (as in CSTB/ULD and PRICKLE1/EPM5);
    cognition preserved — patients maintain meaningful quality of life with adaptation
  CERS1 vs CSTB/ULD key distinctions:
    CERS1: cerebellar ataxia MORE PROMINENT and EARLIER; ULD: tremor/ataxia later in course;
    CERS1: selective MRI cerebellar atrophy; ULD: later cortical/cerebellar changes;
    Mechanism: CERS1 = C18-ceramide/Purkinje; ULD = CSTB/cathepsin inhibitor
  CERS1 vs PRICKLE1/EPM5 key distinctions:
    Both: prominent early cerebellar ataxia, non-fatal, preserved cognition;
    CERS1: lipid/sphingolipid mechanism; PRICKLE1: WNT/PCP pathway;
    CERS1: NO SYNAPSIN link (no LEV mechanistic complementarity);
    CERS1: selective CEREBELLAR atrophy (Purkinje-specific); PRICKLE1: cerebellar+cortical
  Epidemiology: extremely rare; <50 confirmed families worldwide by 2024; AR;
    founder variants in Italian and possibly other Mediterranean populations;
    CERS1 at 19p13.2 — small gene, comprehensive sequencing required for rare variants

CONTRAINDICATIONS IN CERS1-PMEA:
  1. CARBAMAZEPINE / OXCARBAZEPINE / PHENYTOIN — ABSOLUTE CI:
     Na-channel blockers WORSEN action myoclonus in ALL PME types including CERS1-PMEA;
     CERS1-PMEA may be misdiagnosed as cerebellar ataxia syndrome or GGE → CBZ prescribed →
     acute myoclonic deterioration; IV fosphenytoin ABSOLUTE CI for SE → use IV LEV 60 mg/kg
  2. TIAGABINE — ABSOLUTE CI — NCSE + PURKINJE VULNERABILITY:
     GAT-1 blockade → GABA-A desensitisation → NCSE;
     CERS1-PMEA progressive Purkinje cell degeneration amplifies NCSE susceptibility;
     Purkinje cell GABAergic output already compromised by C18-ceramide deficiency
  3. GABAPENTIN / PREGABALIN — HIGH RISK:
     α2δ modulation → paradoxical myoclonic worsening (Crespel 1999 PME series);
     CERS1-PMEA diagnostic trap: cerebellar ataxia + myoclonus may prompt GBP prescription
     for presumed neuropathic/ataxic pain → dangerous myoclonic worsening
  4. LAMOTRIGINE MONOTHERAPY — HIGH RISK:
     Na-channel component → myoclonic worsening; may cause acute myoclonic status;
     NEVER monotherapy; if used as add-on, full video-EEG monitoring required
  5. VIGABATRIN — AVOID:
     GABA-transaminase inhibition → GABA-A desensitisation pattern similar to TGB;
     visual field defects (irreversible) — additional risk in cerebellar disease
"""

